From 521292ff8bc6545671ba07a77d41ea18172d834b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 23:43:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] EXA: accelerate affine-transformed and bilinear
 composites (H2 3D engine)

The UniChrome texture unit can filter bilinearly (HTXnTB, sub-address
0x78, was always written as 0) and takes a texture coordinate per vertex,
so scaled, rotated and skewed Render composites can run on the 3D engine
instead of in software.

- via3DEmitQuad computes the source position as an affine function of
  the destination position for each texture and derives the texture
  coordinates from it. Untransformed output is byte-for-byte what it was.
- A transformed picture without repeat is transparent outside its bounds,
  but the texture unit can only clamp, and only at the power-of-two
  texture size. So the quad is clipped (Sutherland-Hodgman) to the part
  whose samples all fall inside the picture, half a texel in for bilinear,
  and drawn as a triangle fan. That is only correct for operators where a
  transparent source or mask leaves the destination unchanged (Over,
  OverReverse, Atop, Xor, Add); others fall back to software. Compared
  with pixman, the outermost half pixel of a bilinear-filtered picture is
  left out instead of being blended towards transparent.
- New setTexTransform hook sets the transform, the nearest/bilinear filter
  and the clip size for a texture unit; setTexture resets them.
- viaExaPictureSupported() accepts affine transforms with the nearest or
  bilinear filter on pixmaps up to 2048x2048 when the new ExaTransform
  option is on.
- Only the H2 (UniChrome/UniChrome Pro) path is changed; H6 still falls
  back to software for transforms.

Off by default until it has been checked with rendercheck on hardware.
